          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Integrative foundation",
          "content": "### Evidence is strongest when sources of error are different\n\nAn evolutionary reconstruction is not simply a compelling illustration. It is an inference that becomes more convincing when lines of evidence based on distinct observations make compatible predictions. A fossil gives direct evidence that a particular anatomical combination existed by a particular time, subject to correct identification and stratigraphy. A sequence alignment records similarity and difference among sampled molecules, subject to identification of orthology and defensible alignment. A distribution map reflects present occurrence and survey effort, while geology can independently constrain possible routes of dispersal. The strongest historical explanations survive comparisons among these different kinds of evidence rather than forcing each dataset into one preconceived story.\n\nTreat the distinction between *observation*, *inference* and *assumption* as the central practical skill of this unit. Observations might include the measured position of a fossil layer, a counted number of sequence differences or a field record with coordinates. Inferences include placement of a fossil on an evolutionary tree or estimation of a lineage divergence time. Assumptions include the substitution-rate model used for a clock, the completeness of a survey, and the way a morphological character is coded. A clear research report separates the three so readers can evaluate how strongly the evidence bears on the conclusion.\n\n### Constructing a testable historical explanation\n\nImagine a plant lineage presently distributed across two distant mountain ranges. One story proposes that a once continuous range was split by a new lowland region. Another proposes that seeds crossed the lowland after the split. Both explanations can be plausible when written in ordinary language. To discriminate them, collect several independently inherited DNA loci from broad samples of both ranges, inspect the phylogenetic relationships, search for fossils with documented locations, and estimate dates with explicit uncertainty. An inferred split much younger than the barrier is consistent with later dispersal, although time estimates alone rarely settle the question. A split of compatible age also does not prove vicariance; continued dispersal may have occurred.\n\nCharacter coding can change the result just as strongly as sample size. For a fossil skull, coding 'large eye' without defining measurement and developmental stage creates a subjective character. A normalized orbital diameter with a clear landmark and uncertainty is more reproducible. Yet a quantitative character can still be evolutionarily misleading when its distribution is shaped by ecological convergence. Fossil age provides temporal information, not a shortcut around phylogenetic analysis.\n\n### From classroom uncertainty to scientific reporting\n\nFor every evidence claim, report the taxon or population sampling, the feature measured, the alternative hypotheses, and the source of dating or tree uncertainty. Illustrative classroom values must be labeled synthetic; they are useful for learning arithmetic but cannot serve as field evidence. A pattern that agrees with a proposed history provides support only if rival explanations have been examined. A new fossil may refine an age minimum without rewriting every relationship, and a new genome may reveal hybridization that makes a single bifurcating tree incomplete. This is normal scientific revision rather than a defect in evolutionary theory.\n\nA useful final figure for this unit has three panels: observations on a geological time axis, a rooted phylogenetic hypothesis with confidence annotations, and a map showing samples and survey effort. Use different visual encodings for direct evidence, inferred states and hypothetical routes. This prevents a visually appealing reconstruction from quietly treating uncertain arrows as documented fact.",
          "simulations": []
