          "heading": "Integrated investigation 1: Triangulating an island lineage history",
          "content": "**Research aim.** Compare two hypothetical explanations for three related island populations: split of a widespread ancestor as sea level changed, versus repeated colonization by rafting or flight. The investigation is designed for an introductory undergraduate seminar or computational laboratory; its specimens, dates, counts and measurements are hypothetical. Do not represent any result as a published empirical discovery.\n\n### Materials, assumptions and ethical boundaries\n\nA fossil occurrence table with 12 dated observations and independently generated short orthologous alignments for 15 contemporary specimens. Include coordinates, survey years and explicit missing-data codes. Use a spreadsheet or local script to create tables and plots, recording pseudorandom seeds if stochastic simulations are used. Collect no personal genetic data. Do not culture pathogens, collect protected wildlife, disturb habitats or undertake medical tests. Where the real-world analog requires permits, trained staff or ethical approval, use simulation or openly available teaching data instead.\n\n### Step-by-step procedure\n\n1. For **triangulating an island lineage history**, define biological units, sampling frame, time coordinate and outcome variables. Write the competing hypotheses as distinct predictions: Compare two hypothetical explanations for three related island populations: split of a widespread ancestor as sea level changed, versus repeated colonization by rafting or flight. A generic statement that 'evolution occurs' is not a testable outcome.\n2. Assemble the synthetic input table and verify totals. For counts, check whether each observation represents an individual, gene copy, fossil occurrence or independent lineage. For traits, specify scale and units. Leave missing observations explicitly missing rather than silently substituting zero.\n3. Produce a first visualization with individual values visible when practical. Include replicates, not only a pooled mean. Label assumptions, treatment groups and what uncertainty is shown; error bars without a stated definition are ambiguous.\n4. Recalculate the outcome after changing one defensible assumption: sampling effort, effective size, migration, branch rate or measurement error, as appropriate. The change in conclusion is a sensitivity result, not a failure of the exercise.\n5. Write a short report separating observation, model, interpretation and limitation. Include a figure caption, a reproducibility note and one proposed independent test.\n\n### Expected reasoning, not prescribed results\n\nIf an older fossil occurs on the proposed source island, its age can revise a minimum occupancy date but cannot alone establish a successful dispersal route. In this synthetic scenario, a simple map and a proposed tree disagree about dispersal direction when the oldest material is in a poorly sampled area. Resist presenting a single calculated value as the only outcome consistent with the mechanism. Explain what a failed prediction would require you to reconsider: an incorrect method, an unrealistic assumption or a genuinely unsupported biological hypothesis. Negative results are informative when sampling was capable of detecting the predicted difference.\n\n### Troubleshooting the inference\n\nAsk whether the same story could be produced by convergence, selective preservation or a different geographic sampling scheme. If the result rests on absence, quantify the effort that would have been required to detect the missing organism. Pseudoreplication is a major threat when multiple measurements from one biological unit are treated as independent organisms or populations. Similarly, sampling several fossils from one well-exposed outcrop may not represent equal coverage across geological intervals. Identify the true replication unit and justify the comparator. If simulations use the same seed or parameter configuration, discuss whether the apparently repeated outcome is truly independent.\n\n### Submission and further questions\n\nSubmit a hypothesis table, methods summary, results figure with labeled axes, numerical worked example, explanation of assumptions and a 150-word limitations statement. How might discovery probability and geological reconstruction create the observed disagreement? For extension, describe one observation that would distinguish the leading hypothesis from a realistic competing mechanism, and state how much uncertainty would remain even if that observation agreed with the prediction.",
