          "heading": "Integrated investigation 8: Rapid-change monitoring with ethical limits",
          "content": "**Research aim.** Assess whether a simulated increase in frequency of an urban-associated trait reflects genetic evolution, immigration or phenotypic plasticity. The investigation is designed for an introductory undergraduate seminar or computational laboratory; its specimens, dates, counts and measurements are hypothetical. Do not represent any result as a published empirical discovery.\n\n### Materials, assumptions and ethical boundaries\n\nTime-series allele counts, migration estimates, repeated phenotypes at paired urban and rural sites, and a short common-garden exercise performed on fictitious observations. Use a spreadsheet or local script to create tables and plots, recording pseudorandom seeds if stochastic simulations are used. Collect no personal genetic data. Do not culture pathogens, collect protected wildlife, disturb habitats or undertake medical tests. Where the real-world analog requires permits, trained staff or ethical approval, use simulation or openly available teaching data instead.\n\n### Step-by-step procedure\n\n1. For **rapid-change monitoring with ethical limits**, define biological units, sampling frame, time coordinate and outcome variables. Write the competing hypotheses as distinct predictions: Assess whether a simulated increase in frequency of an urban-associated trait reflects genetic evolution, immigration or phenotypic plasticity. A generic statement that 'evolution occurs' is not a testable outcome.\n2. Assemble the synthetic input table and verify totals. For counts, check whether each observation represents an individual, gene copy, fossil occurrence or independent lineage. For traits, specify scale and units. Leave missing observations explicitly missing rather than silently substituting zero.\n3. Produce a first visualization with individual values visible when practical. Include replicates, not only a pooled mean. Label assumptions, treatment groups and what uncertainty is shown; error bars without a stated definition are ambiguous.\n4. Recalculate the outcome after changing one defensible assumption: sampling effort, effective size, migration, branch rate or measurement error, as appropriate. The change in conclusion is a sensitivity result, not a failure of the exercise.\n5. Write a short report separating observation, model, interpretation and limitation. Include a figure caption, a reproducibility note and one proposed independent test.\n\n### Expected reasoning, not prescribed results\n\nPopulation growth, allele-frequency change and phenotypic change are different quantities; none should be used as a proxy for another without justification. In this synthetic scenario, both trait values and census counts increase, yet the frequency of the focal genetic variant changes little once immigration is considered. Resist presenting a single calculated value as the only outcome consistent with the mechanism. Explain what a failed prediction would require you to reconsider: an incorrect method, an unrealistic assumption or a genuinely unsupported biological hypothesis. Negative results are informative when sampling was capable of detecting the predicted difference.\n\n### Troubleshooting the inference\n\nSeparate numerical evidence of an allele shift from the ethical or practical decision being considered. If a proposed intervention could cause harm, use synthetic data, published observations or supervised specialists instead of independent field experimentation. Pseudoreplication is a major threat when multiple measurements from one biological unit are treated as independent organisms or populations. Similarly, sampling several fossils from one well-exposed outcrop may not represent equal coverage across geological intervals. Identify the true replication unit and justify the comparator. If simulations use the same seed or parameter configuration, discuss whether the apparently repeated outcome is truly independent.\n\n### Submission and further questions\n\nSubmit a hypothesis table, methods summary, results figure with labeled axes, numerical worked example, explanation of assumptions and a 150-word limitations statement. What additional longitudinal evidence could justify a stronger claim of rapid local adaptation? For extension, describe one observation that would distinguish the leading hypothesis from a realistic competing mechanism, and state how much uncertainty would remain even if that observation agreed with the prediction.",
